# Resolve one Michigan parcel's identity block: route by trust path, fetch,
# normalize (dropping owner names), find a point inside it, ask the statewide
# layer what township that point is in, and join the two halves.
#
# The run has finished when `resolveIdentity` has produced the identity block.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FRICTION (#4, RUN GRANULARITY -- the structural one you asked about)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `input: ParcelRequest` says one parcel is one run. That is NOT blue-ribbon's
# unit of work, and the mismatch is not cosmetic.
#
# The real ETL (apps/etl/src/batch.ts) runs a Blue Ribbon corridor: 5 counties,
# 17,839 parcels, ONE DuckDB table, ONE parquet file, ONE manifest.json whose
# cardCount is 17839. The final step is `COPY cards TO ... (FORMAT PARQUET)` --
# a fold over every parcel that was resolved.
#
# Neither granularity works:
#
#   ONE RUN PER PARCEL (what I wrote). 17,839 runs. The export has no home:
#   it is a fold across correlation ids, and "nothing joins across correlation
#   ids, by design". So the parquet write is simply NOT A WEIR NODE. It sits
#   outside the program, reading the log. That may be the right answer -- it
#   is a materialized view over the log, which is exactly what the readme says
#   tables are -- but it means the thing the pipeline EXISTS TO PRODUCE is
#   outside the model, and `output:`/`terminals:` describe the end of one
#   parcel rather than the end of the job. A run that "finishes" 17,839 times
#   and has produced nothing a human wanted is a strange definition of done.
#
#   ONE RUN PER CORRIDOR. Then the export is a `gather` and fits beautifully.
#   But: the spread is 17,839 wide; one failed township lookup out of 3,265
#   leaves residue and the whole corridor exits non-zero; the log for one run
#   is millions of entries; and pagination still cannot be gathered (see
#   fetchParcelPage.node #1). blue-ribbon's real semantics are "that parcel
#   gets a null with a note, the other 3,264 still produce cards" -- a gather
#   that tolerates holes and records them.
#
# MY ACTUAL ANSWER: blue-ribbon is ONE run per corridor, and weir cannot
# express it today because of the gather-with-holes gap, not because of
# anything about correlation ids. The per-parcel framing below is a workaround
# I chose to get something runnable, and I want to be clear that it is a
# retreat rather than a modelling decision.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The diamond, drawn, because the tree below obscures it:
#
#   routeCounty --oneOf--> directCountyFetch  [zone: county-gis] ----+
#                     \                                              |
#                      +-> proxiedCountyFetch [zone: third-party] ---+
#                                                                    |
#                                                          RawParcelFeature
#                                                                    |
#                                                            normalizeParcel
#                                                              /          \
#                                              ParcelCentroid             |
#                                                    |                    |
#                                      townshipResolution [zone:state-gis]|
#                                                    |                    |
#                                            TownshipLookup      NormalizedParcel
#                                                    \                    /
#                                                     +--> resolveIdentity
#
# Note the right-hand arc: NormalizedParcel feeds the join directly AND is the
# ancestor of the other input. That is the lineage question flagged in
# resolveIdentity.node, and it is the shape I am least sure weir supports.
zone: server
input: ParcelRequest
output: ParcelIdentity
terminals:
  - resolveIdentity
wiring:
  routeCounty:
    then:
      directCountyFetch:
        then:
          normalizeParcel:
            then:
              parcelCentroid:
                then:
                  townshipResolution:
                    then:
                      resolveIdentity: {}
              # The short side of the diamond: the normalized parcel is also
              # an input to the join, alongside the township derived from it.
              resolveIdentity: {}

      proxiedCountyFetch:
        then:
          normalizeParcel: {}
